-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 false] [17592186235392 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nXZ+YZ=(X+Y)Z.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =XZ+YZ \\\\  & =(X+Y)Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 true] [17592186235393 1074 "In part 1, we found that the matrix representation of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the field under the action of $T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -21 & -21 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 14 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-9,4),$ $(-21,14),$ $(-21,10),$ and $(-9,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7430fd0c1e3a4c94e951eb1cf133e5c155f7a7b605a46b4b2272bf30fbb688e2.png)" 13194139535475 17592186045443 false] [17592186235393 1074 "In part 1, we found that the matrix representation of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the field under the action of $T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -21 & -21 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 14 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-9,4),$ $(-21,14),$ $(-21,10),$ and $(-9,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7430fd0c1e3a4c94e951eb1cf133e5c155f7a7b605a46b4b2272bf30fbb688e2.png)" 13194139535475 17592186045443 true] [17592186235395 1074 "In part 1, we found that the matrix representation of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of the larger recreational area:\n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of the smaller recreational area, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)(-3)-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-15 & -15 & -21 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 15 & 21 \\\\ 18 & 24 & 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordin