ion is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 false] [17592186244031 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 true] [17592186244033 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 false] [17592186244033 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139535475 17592186045443